    "q-63873": {
      "dom_id": "step-q63873",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A park is estimated to have $11\\,000$ rabbits. If their population decreases by $2%$ every year, what will the population of rabbits be approximately in ten years?",
      "worked_solution": "The number $2%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.02.$ Therefore, a decrease of $2%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.02=0.98\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, there will be $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)$ rabbits.\n\n- After $2$ years, there will be approximately $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)^{2}$ rabbits.\n\n- After $3$ years, there will be approximately $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)^{3}$ rabbits.\n\n- $…$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that there will be approximately $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)^{10}$ rabbits in $10$ years.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
